<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">FOO consensus loop.
An arbitrary set of agents <math id="S2.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> receives
the user task, produces candidate answers, cross-critiques
peers, and feeds all critiques to one or more harmonisers
<math id="S2.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Harmonisers synthesise the feedback; agents revise
and the loop continues until a convergence criterion is
met. The design allows any number or type of agents and
any custom instruction set, making the architecture task-
and model-agnostic.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> FOO consensus loop (model-agnostic)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            user task <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>; agent set <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>; convergence test

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            final harmonised answer <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            broadcast <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to every <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and collect initial answers

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">repeat</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for all</span> agent <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
               supply <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> with “find flaws in” <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\text{answers of }\mathcal{A}\setminus\{a\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>answers of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\text{answers of }\mathcal{A}\setminus\{a\}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
               receive critique <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              harmoniser(s) <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> aggregate <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C_{a}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C_{a}\}</annotation></semantics></math> into judgement <math id="alg1.l9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for all</span> non-harmoniser <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               regenerate answer <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on <math id="alg1.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">until</span> convergence test satisfied

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> final <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation note.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement the loop in <span id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Python 3.11</span> with <span id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">asyncio</span>
concurrency; each agent call is an HTTP request to a hosted LLM endpoint
(e.g. OpenAI, Anthropic). Unless stated otherwise, the experimental
pool comprises a single <em id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harmoniser</em> (the most capable model
available) and, for <em id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">each</em> back-end engine under test, two
specialist agents sampled at temperatures 0.1 and 0.9. The protocol
executes one broadcast round plus a minimum of three consensus rounds,
so every query triggers</p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{cost}\;=\;4\times|A|\quad\text{API calls}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>cost</mtext><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mn> 4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>API calls</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{cost}\;=\;4\times|A|\quad\text{API calls},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A|</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of agents (harmoniser + specialists).
For example, with one engine (<math id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A|=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A|=3</annotation></semantics></math>) the loop issues
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 3=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 3=12</annotation></semantics></math> calls; adding engines or additional specialist roles
scales the cost linearly.
All configuration files and source code are publicly available at
<a href="https://github.com/biomathematicus/foo" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/biomathematicus/foo</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> report that state-of-the-art LLMs answer medical questions incorrectly in <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">26-61 %</span> of cases, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> show that the same models’ built-in self-evaluation (<span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SELF-EVAL</span>) flags invalidations with modestly better than chance (AUROC <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.51" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.51</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.51</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.59</annotation></semantics></math> on TruthfulQA and BioGEN). Taken together, these studies indicate a realistic operating regime in which</p>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\;&gt;\;q,\quad\text{and therefore}\quad\pi_{f}^{\text{single}}\;=\;\frac{p+\lambda}{p+\lambda+q}\;\gtrsim\;0.50," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and therefore</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi><mtext>single</mtext></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">≳</mo><mn> 0.50</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\;&gt;\;q,\quad\text{and therefore}\quad\pi_{f}^{\text{single}}\;=\;\frac{p+\lambda}{p+\lambda+q}\;\gtrsim\;0.50,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">i.e. the model fabricates new errors faster than it can repair them
internally (“<em id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invalidation-dominant</em>”).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Single-network baseline ‣ 3 Results ‣ The Flaws of Others: An LLM-driven Framework for Scientific Knowledge Production" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> lists the numerical choices used in the simulations and the manuscript, together with their provenance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Single-network baseline</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the calibrated triple <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, the single-network
emergent-invalidation model (Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2.2 (Single-network invalidation with fabrication). ‣ Update equations. ‣ 2.3.2 Single-Network Emergent Invalidation Model ‣ 2.3 Modeling Discursive Networks ‣ 2 Methods ‣ The Flaws of Others: An LLM-driven Framework for Scientific Knowledge Production" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) predicts</p>
<table id="S3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}^{\text{single}}\;=\;\frac{0.02+0.055}{0.02+0.055+0.05}\;=\;0.60,\qquad\pi_{r}^{\text{single}}=0.40." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi><mtext>single</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.02</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.055</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0.02</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.055</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mn> 0.60</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mtext>single</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.40</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}^{\text{single}}\;=\;\frac{0.02+0.055}{0.02+0.055+0.05}\;=\;0.60,\qquad\pi_{r}^{\text{single}}=0.40.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Monte-Carlo simulation (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOO_Single_Network.py</span>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> runs, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> steps)
converges to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{f}(100)=0.597\pm 0.018" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.597</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.018</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{f}(100)=0.597\pm 0.018</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the analytic
fixed-point within sampling error.</p>
</div>
<figure id="T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="T4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Calibrated parameter set. All hazards are <em id="T4.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per statement</em>.</span></figcaption>
<table id="T4.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="T4.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="T4.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">Symbol</th>
<th id="T4.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">Value</th>
<th id="T4.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">Model role</th>
<th id="T4.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">
<span id="T4.6.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="T4.6.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">Empirical justification</span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="T4.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="T4.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;"><math id="T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="T4.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;"><math id="T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="T4.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">intrinsic corruption (<math id="T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!\to\!f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!\to\!f</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="T4.6.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">
<span id="T4.6.2.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="T4.6.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">Ordinary slip rate, kept below <math id="T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> so that internal repair
is non-trivial.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="T4.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="T4.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;"><math id="T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="T4.6.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;"><math id="T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="T4.6.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">intrinsic self-repair (<math id="T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\!\to\!r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\!\to\!r</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="T4.6.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">
<span id="T4.6.3.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="T4.6.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">Chosen to reflect SELF-EVAL’s weak but non-zero success
(<math id="T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AUROC}\approx 0.55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AUROC</mtext><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AUROC}\approx 0.55</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="T4.6.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="T4.6.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;"><math id="T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="T4.6.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;"><math id="T4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.055" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.055</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.055</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="T4.6.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">fabrication / hallucination (<math id="T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!\to\!f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!\to\!f</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="T4.6.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">
<span id="T4.6.4.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="T4.6.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">Upper end of the 26-61 % error range implies
<math id="T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;q</annotation></semantics></math>; solving <math id="T4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}^{*}\approx 0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}^{*}\approx 0.60</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="T4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
with the <math id="T4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math> above gives <math id="T4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.055" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.055</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.055</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="T4.6.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="T4.6.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;"><math id="T4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="T4.6.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;"><math id="T4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.19</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="T4.6.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">cross-network detection (<math id="T4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\!\to\!r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\!\to\!r</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="T4.6.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 5.0pt;">
<span id="T4.6.5.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="T4.6.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">Set so that <math id="T4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda/d=p/(p+q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda/d=p/(p+q)</annotation></semantics></math>, placing the dual system
just inside the truth-dominant region (Lemma
<a href="#S2.Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 2.4 (Cross-network detection lowers falsehood prevalence). ‣ Stationary false-statement shares. ‣ 2.4 Single- vs. Cross-Network Models with Invalidation ‣ 2 Methods ‣ The Flaws of Others: An LLM-driven Framework for Scientific Knowledge Production" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>).</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2507.06565v1/FOO_Single-Network.png" id="S3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/334;" width="476" height="334" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2507.06565v1/FOO_Dual-Network.png" id="S3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/399;" width="476" height="399" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">(a)<span id="S3.F3.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Single-network invalidation dynamics for <math id="S3.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> Monte-Carlo
realizations of <math id="S3.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> time steps. The blue curve shows the mean number of
actors endorsing statement <math id="S3.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (treated as <em id="S3.F3.8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">true</em>); the red curve
shows the mean number endorsing statement <math id="S3.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (treated as
<em id="S3.F3.8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">false</em>). Shaded bands depict the point-wise <math id="S3.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence
intervals. Dashed horizontal lines mark the analytical asymptotes
<math id="S3.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}^{*}=1-P_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}^{*}=1-P_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> predicted by the discrete two-state
Markov model with flip probabilities <math id="S3.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{21}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span>(b)<span id="S3.F3.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Mean trajectories of true (blue) and false (red) statement
counts for two interacting discursive networks over <math id="S3.F3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> time steps,
obtained from <math id="S3.F3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> Monte-Carlo runs of the stochastic model in
Eqs. <a href="#S2.E5" title="In Normalised update equations. ‣ 2.3.3 Cross-Network Invalidation-Detection Model ‣ 2.3 Modeling Discursive Networks ‣ 2 Methods ‣ The Flaws of Others: An LLM-driven Framework for Scientific Knowledge Production" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-<a href="#S2.E6" title="In Normalised update equations. ‣ 2.3.3 Cross-Network Invalidation-Detection Model ‣ 2.3 Modeling Discursive Networks ‣ 2 Methods ‣ The Flaws of Others: An LLM-driven Framework for Scientific Knowledge Production" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Shaded bands show the point-wise
<math id="S3.F3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence intervals. Dashed horizontal lines mark the analytical
asymptotes <math id="S3.F3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 2.3 (Dual-network invalidation propagation). ‣ Normalised update equations. ‣ 2.3.3 Cross-Network Invalidation-Detection Model ‣ 2.3 Modeling Discursive Networks ‣ 2 Methods ‣ The Flaws of Others: An LLM-driven Framework for Scientific Knowledge Production" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>. The upper subplot belongs to
Network 1; the lower to Network 2.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, false statements accumulate faster than they are
self-repaired; the long-run false share sits squarely in the empirical
band reported by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dual-network architecture</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Coupling two identical networks via dual-detection <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> augments each
false statement with an independent external repair hazard. The fixed
point from Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 2.4 (Cross-network detection lowers falsehood prevalence). ‣ Stationary false-statement shares. ‣ 2.4 Single- vs. Cross-Network Models with Invalidation ‣ 2 Methods ‣ The Flaws of Others: An LLM-driven Framework for Scientific Knowledge Production" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> becomes</p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}^{\text{cross}}\;=\;\frac{\lambda}{d}\;=\;0.29,\qquad\pi_{r}^{\text{cross}}=0.71," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi><mtext>cross</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mn> 0.29</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mtext>cross</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.71</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}^{\text{cross}}\;=\;\frac{\lambda}{d}\;=\;0.29,\qquad\pi_{r}^{\text{cross}}=0.71,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">halving</em> the falsehood prevalence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Simulations (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOO_Dual_Network.py</span>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> runs, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> steps) yield
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{f}^{N_{1}}(200)=0.286\pm 0.015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>200</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.286</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.015</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{f}^{N_{1}}(200)=0.286\pm 0.015</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{f}^{N_{2}}(200)=0.289\pm 0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>200</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.289</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.014</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{f}^{N_{2}}(200)=0.289\pm 0.014</annotation></semantics></math>, again validating theory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-network detection mimics techniques such as retrieval-augmented
generation, ensemble adjudication or human post-editing, all of which
contribute an external hazard <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Once <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough that
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)</annotation></semantics></math>, the system crosses from an
invalidation-dominant regime (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}\approx 0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}\approx 0.60</annotation></semantics></math>) to a truth-dominant
one (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}\approx 0.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.29</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}\approx 0.29</annotation></semantics></math>). The 51 % relative reduction
quantifies (for the chosen calibration) the factual-gains practitioners
can expect from pairing a generative LLM with an independent verifier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Internal verification by a single model proves inadequate: whenever the
fabrication hazard <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the intrinsic self-repair rate
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the single-network equilibrium reproduces the high invalidation
rates documented in current benchmarks. Introducing an external source
of scrutiny changes the picture. In our calibration, raising the
cross-detection probability to a modest <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\approx 0.19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.19</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\approx 0.19</annotation></semantics></math> is already
enough to halve the long-run share of false statements. The decisive
quantity is the ratio <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda/d</annotation></semantics></math>; system builders can treat it as a
design dial, increasing <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (or equivalently reducing <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>)
until the condition <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)</annotation></semantics></math> is met and the network
operates in a truth-dominant regime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Conclusions and Outlook</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study deliberately replaces the fashionable term
<em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hallucination</em> with the broader concept of
<em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invalidation</em>. Whereas “hallucination” suggests a purely
accidental slip in the model’s internal perception, the data reveal a
richer spectrum of invalidations (failure modes) that includes strategic prompt manipulation, chain-of-thought drift and the simple inheritance of errors from flawed training corpora. All of these mechanisms manifest in the observable metric that matters to users (the production of false statements) so the single hazard rate <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is most naturally interpreted as an <em id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invalidation rate</em>. The shift in vocabulary is therefore more than semantics: it aligns theoretical parameters with the phenomena actually counted in benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A model that relies only on its
own self-repair routines is destined to retain a majority of
invalid statements, in line with the error rates that practitioners see
in real deployments. Calibrating <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda,p," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,p,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to the medical QA study of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> and the self-evaluation results of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> places the isolated network firmly in the
region <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;q</annotation></semantics></math>. Analytically, this is the
<em id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invalidation-dominant</em> regime; numerically, it implies an
equilibrium false-statement share of about sixty per cent. Monte-Carlo
experiments with our single-network simulator confirm this prediction,
ending every run within a few points of the theoretical
<math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}^{\text{single}}\simeq 0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi><mtext>single</mtext></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><mn>0.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}^{\text{single}}\simeq 0.60</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The picture changes as soon as a second, independent verifier is coupled
to the generator. In our formalism that coupling introduces a new hazard <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> which gives every false statement an additional chance per
time-step to be detected and corrected. When <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is tuned so that the
inequality <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)</annotation></semantics></math> holds, the system crosses a critical
boundary and enters a <em id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">truth-dominant</em> regime. Using the parameter values inferred from the two empirical studies and choosing the cross-detection rate <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to satisfy <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)</annotation></semantics></math>, the dual-network architecture is predicted to lower the steady-state false-statement share to about <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>29</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29\,\%</annotation></semantics></math>, a reduction of
roughly fifty per cent compared with an isolated network. The numerical results presented here signal the universal applicability of the FOO algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although the mathematics treats <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> as a single scalar, the concept
encompasses several concrete engineering choices. Retrieval-augmented
generation raises <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> by surrounding the model with authoritative
passages that expose contradictions. Model-ensemble adjudication raises
it further by combining the diverse priors of independently trained
models; uncorrelated errors rarely agree, so the aggregate chance that a
falsehood slips through diminishes rapidly. Even higher values of
<math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> become attainable when a human editor is placed in the loop,
although latency and cost then become limiting factors. Our hazard
model quantifies these trade-offs: for any target false-statement
tolerance one can compute a required detection rate, and thus budget the
amount of human or automated scrutiny that must be applied.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In short, the slogan “trust, then verify” admits a precise operational
reading: raise the external detection hazard until the inequality
<math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda/d&lt;p/(p+q)</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied. At that point a network of language
models can operate in a truth-dominant regime, even though every
individual generator by itself remains prone to invalidation. What
began as an abstract Markov model thus delivers a practical design rule
for building safer language-technology pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Just as people are quicker to spot another person’s mistakes than their own, a language model is often a sharper critic of a peer’s text than of its own output. The asymmetry stems from each model’s training objective: during generation it maximises local fluency rather than global factuality, so a well-phrased falsehood slips through unchallenged. When the same model is asked only to evaluate an already-written passage, fluency is no longer the bottleneck; the task collapses to checking claims against the knowledge stored in its training corpus. Verification is therefore easier, and cross-model review can expose errors that the original authoring pass left intact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The significance of these findings extends beyond artificial systems.
Invalidation dynamics of the same mathematical form could govern human
conversation, peer review and social-media fact-checking. Recognising
this shared structure invites a unified research agenda that links
network science, cognitive psychology and algorithmic governance. Our
future work will explore heterogeneous hazards at the level of
individual actors, time-varying detection capacities that respond to
workload, and live A/B tests that recover hazard estimates directly from
production chat traffic. A deeper ethical analysis will also be
required, because the push toward smaller <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> competes with
privacy constraints, energy budgets and the carbon footprint of
large-scale verification.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">We have entered a new cultural mode. It affects scientific production.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hazard model introduced here traces one measurable thread (how invalidations arise and are repaired) but it sits inside a larger transformation. Generative models are not merely assistive tools; they mark a transition to what has been called <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Literatronica</em>, a media regime in which composition is recursive, co-constructed and software-mediated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. The sentence, paragraph, and page, units that stabilised only after centuries of cultural negotiation, cede agency to a system that writes without remembering and edits while it speaks. Invalidations therefore matter twice: first as a practical reliability metric, and second as a probe of how the new medium itself behaves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have crossed a cultural threshold: language is no longer crafted solely by human hands and minds, but is continuously co-composed with machines. Large-language-model systems do not “assist” writing; they repurpose it. Authorship dissolves into a live dialogue between human intention and algorithmic completion, with sentences generated, revised, and re-externalised in the same breath. Because the practice itself has changed, the evaluative yardsticks built for solitary, page-bound prose (originality scores, citation counts, style rubrics, etc.) do no longer capture what is happening on the screen. A new cultural mode demands new methods, training, expectations, and metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Looking forward, the research programme must widen from
token-level truthfulness to <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">medium-level dynamics</span>. Three paths
stand out:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cultural assimilation.</span> Our most pressing need is an ethical framework to harmonize societal values with this new reality. We need conventions,
interfaces and pedagogies that make continuous, model-mediated
composition legible, trustworthy, and fair.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric redesign.</span> Benchmarks rooted in solitary
authorship and static text can no longer capture quality in a
live, co-creative medium. New metrics should score how a
statement evolves under iterative detection and repair, not
just its instantaneous truth value.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Governance of agency.</span> When the medium itself
“acts on the message,” responsibility diffuses across
designers, deployers and end-users. Future hazard models
must therefore integrate economic incentives, interface
affordances and policy constraints alongside the technical
parameters <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q,\lambda,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q,\lambda,d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Seen through this lens, the ideas discussed in this manuscript form a
prototype for broader cultural ratios that will emerge as society
learns, <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">once again</em>, to write in a fundamentally new medium.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This mansucript was produced with the workflow described in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. The author supplied the core ideas, argument
structure, and foundational technical content; large-language-model assistance was used for ideation, verification, text drafting and revision, and software implementation. The FOO Algorithm was executed with five LLM agents working in parallel (two Anthropic Claude 4.5, two OpenAI GPT-4.1, and one OpenAI GPT-o1). All responsibility for the claims herein remain with the author. The logs are provided as supplementary material,</p>
</div>
</section>
